==========================================================================================
CONTROL (case 4): the DECLARED-EXCITATION problem, for contrast
==========================================================================================
  If orthogonality is required only for ONE given excitation rather than for every
  excitation, the ten MATRIX conditions collapse to ten SCALAR conditions, because
  each becomes a single number summed over the record. Ten scalars on eighteen real
  unknowns leaves an 8-dimensional solution space at least. This is the control: it
  must be large, or the framing itself is wrong.

  declared-excitation null space dimension: 9 of 18

==========================================================================================
VERDICT
==========================================================================================
  The only G satisfying all ten conditions at every scheduling point is G = 0.
  IMPOSSIBILITY PROVEN, for this baseline and this parameterisation:

    No linear added dynamics, and no memoryless (M_a, C_a, K_a) perturbation,
    is orthogonal to the baseline parameter sensitivities for EVERY excitation.

  Consequences, and they are the useful part:
   1. Orthogonality by construction, independent of the experiment, is NOT
      available. Any orthogonal addition must come with a declared excitation.
   2. The contrast with case 4 says where the freedom actually lives: an
      8-dimensional solution space once the excitation is fixed, against nothing
      when it is not. Addition design and excitation design are ONE problem.
   3. This is very likely why Gyorok et al. Sect. 5.2 concede their construction
      breaks once the regressor carries past outputs and say a different data
      acquisition would be required. The obstruction is structural, not an
      artefact of their example.
